// A **delegating factory** of a generic type now records too — the case the fresh-constructor rule
// deliberately cannot cover on its own.
//
// `relay` is NOT a fresh constructor (`compute_fresh_constructors` requires every `return` to hand
// back a bare literal; this one returns another call's result), so its own call site may not stamp the
// object: the value it returns need not be freshly allocated by *this* call, and re-tagging a possibly
// shared instance is what the fresh-constructor rule exists to forbid.
//
// It works anyway, and through the other end: the `Inner.new(raw)` INSIDE `relay` is a fresh
// constructor at a checked position (`relay`'s declared return), and `T` there is `relay`'s own class
// parameter — so it takes the hidden slot, and the caller's instantiation reaches the literal that
// really is fresh. The tag lands on the object at the one point it is provably unshared.
//
// Without an annotation to pin the instantiation this is E0023 at check time (nothing determines `T`),
// which is the boundary `class_type_param_self_less_uninferable` pins for the same channel.
// expect: stdout "todos:Todo"
// expect: exit 0
struct Todo {
    id: int
}
class Inner<T> {
    pub tbl: string

    fn new(tbl: string): Inner<T> {
        return Inner { tbl: tbl };
    }

    pub fn relay(raw: string): Inner<T> {
        return Inner.new(raw);
    }

    pub fn model(): string {
        return self.tbl ~ ":" ~ type_name::<T>();
    }
}
i: Inner<Todo> = Inner.relay("todos");
echo i.model();
